In article <hamkins.750391303@grinch>, hamkins@geisel.csl.uiuc.edu (Jon
Hamkins) wrote:

> Can someone tell me what "conduct of speech" means, and how is it
> different from the content of speech?

There is a theory that speech can't be regulated, whereas conduct can, but
that some speech is really conduct, and can hence be regulated.

So, for example, if a crowd is standing around you brandishing baseball
bats, and I say "Gee, it would be a good idea to beat someone's head in
about now, theoretically speaking" then that is "conduct" speech, and not
protected.

Or, if I say "I think Anjelica Huston is sexy," and several weeks later a
female student is raped, then that is "conduct" speech (because I have
created an atmosphere where women are just objects, signalling to men that
it's fine to rape them).

The really fun part is that Mary Ellen gets to decide what conduct speech
is during your hearing.  If the sexual harassment policy is any guide, this
vagueness is DELIBERATE, so that the University has as much "discretion"
(read, "arbitrary power") as it needs to accomplish its goals.

At least the UI senate showed some sense on both these issues.
-- 
