I don't doubt that there is a lot of PC activity on these campuses, but I 
suspect we're not hearing the whole story on some of these anecdotes.

>o Stanford for skimming over $200M in government contract money to, among other
>things, help 'recreate' the image of a professor who killed himself after 
>being unmasked as a pedophile ('adherent of an alternative lifestyle')

This item makes me question the integrity of this list.  We all know that 
Stanford was nailed for playing games with the government money they got 
for overhead on research projects.  But surely that isn't evidence of 
political correctness.  

So what the author of this book has done is to combine that fact with 
something that sounds PCish, in order to make the implication that a 
serious amount of money was spent on this "image recreation".  Exactly 
what does "image recreation" mean?  

Did they name a building after him?  Hire a PR firm to clean up his 
image?  Exactly what are we talking about here?  

Who made the statement calling him an "adherent of an alternative 
lifestyle" in reference to the pedophilia?  The university?  The 
implication there is that the university viewed his pedophilia as just 
another lifestyle choice, but is that true? 

>o U. of Arizona, which placed an absolute ban on the hiring of white male
>professors

This is a little hard to believe.  I'm sure they went out of their way to 
hire minorities and women, but I have trouble believing they put an 
absolute ban on white males.  For how long?  

>o CCNY for misappropriation of half a million dollars in student funds to,
>among other things, send the sister of the student body president on a trip to
>Africa 

Under what circumstances?  Was it a research trip?  Sounds to me like it 
was (at worst) a case of a student using his influence to inappropriately 
get his sister on a university-funded trip.  This sort of stuff happens 
all the time.  I'm not saying it's right, but labeling it PC seems like a 
bit of a stretch.

>o SUNY/Binghamton for banning blood drives on campus during the Gulf War
>because the Red Cross policy of excluding as donors people suspected as 
>having AIDS was "racist"

Does the Red Cross really exclude people based on race?  Again, this 
sounds fishy to me. 

>o U of Cal. for enforcing a policy of student admission on a strict racial
>quota basis, regardless of personal qualification or capability

That sounds like a reasonable case to include.

>You get the picture. The book is well worth reading (and very well written to
>boot).

Yeah, I get the picture.  Instead of focusing on real issues (quotas, 
university admittance, speech codes) the authors use a lot of anecdotes 
that sound like a lot of relevant facts are left out.  The kind of slimy 
tactics you generally hear on talk radio (both on the left and right).  

Can anyone on these campuses provide some more information about these cases?

